[
    {
        "content": "<p>I was experimenting with pretty printers for <code>#eval (0 : W.Point)</code> for points of a Weierstrass curve <code>W</code>, where the carrier is a quotient of <code>Fin 3 → R</code> for a ring <code>R</code>, and I encountered this segfault for v4.35.0-rc3 reproducible in live.lean-lang:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">unsafe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">unsafeCast</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Quot</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">True</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I don't understand the issue, but maybe this is useful to someone here. I asked Claude and here is what it says.</p>\n<p>The compiled code for <code>c</code> drops the application <code>v 0</code> and returns the closure itself:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"bp\">._</span><span class=\"n\">closed_0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">obj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x_1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">obj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"bp\">._</span><span class=\"n\">lam_0</span><span class=\"bp\">._</span><span class=\"n\">boxed</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ret</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x_1</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tobj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x_1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">obj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"bp\">._</span><span class=\"n\">closed_0</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">inc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x_1</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ret</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x_1</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>#eval</code> then prints that closure as a Nat and crashes. Without the <code>Quot.mk (unsafeCast (fun i =&gt; i + 10))</code>, it prints <code>10</code> correctly. With a named <code>def f : Nat → Nat</code> in place of the lambda it still crashes. This matters beyond toy code because Mathlib's <code>Quot.unquot</code> is exactly <code>unsafeCast</code>. Unsafe <code>Repr</code> instances built on it can crash on closed values whose class is visibly a <code>Quot.mk</code> of a function. While <code>unsafeCast</code> comes with no guarantees in general, here the cast is between types with the same runtime representation (<code>Quot</code> is erased), which is exactly what <code>Quot.unquot</code> relies on.</p>\n<p>The workaround for the Weierstrass curve case is to move the body into a separate <code>@[noinline] unsafe def</code> taking the representative.</p>",
        "id": 628034519,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1790763616
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14719\">lean4#14719</a>. But in general, I'd avoid <code>unsafeCast</code> as much as possible in favor of other alternatives. In this case, <code>unquot</code> should be defined as <code>q.lift id lcProof</code> instead of <code>unsafeCast q</code>.</p>",
        "id": 628053622,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1790768705
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/44354\">#44354</a> should fix this particular case from mathlib's side</p>",
        "id": 628058388,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1790769816
    },
